From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: read the driver's display timings at every present

The records VK_GOOGLE_display_timing keeps were read only when a frame
was timed by counting refreshes (0014). A frame timed at the core's
rate (0018) never asked, so on a variable refresh display, the case
that matters, nothing read them: the driver's queue dropped the oldest,
a frame falling back to the refresh count (menu, fast-forward) started
from the oldest eight it then got, and a layer that sees the records
through these reads, MangoHud, saw none.

Read them at every present with display timing, whichever way it is
timed. A read costs a lock and a copy of the records since the last.

Measured on SM8550 (Retroid Pocket Nova), Super Mario World, variable
refresh, three runs each: phase of the panel against the targets 6.3,
7.5, 8.6 ms before and 6.2, 4.0, 8.0 ms after, late frames 12 to 16 and
refreshes without a commit 2 to 4 in both, so the timing is unchanged;
MangoHud's display intervals went from none to every frame.

Signed-off-by: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
---
diff --git a/gfx/common/vulkan_common.c b/gfx/common/vulkan_common.c
--- a/gfx/common/vulkan_common.c
+++ b/gfx/common/vulkan_common.c
@@ -3792,6 +3792,13 @@
    /* An ID per present, so the timing query below can name it. */
    if (vk->display_timing_supported && vk->display_timing_query)
    {
+      /* Read what the driver has timed at every present, however this
+       * one is timed. Frames timed at the core's rate never asked, so
+       * the records went unread: a frame that falls back to counting
+       * refreshes (menu, fast-forward) started from a stale present, and
+       * a layer that sees the records only through these reads
+       * (MangoHud) saw none. */
+      vulkan_poll_present_timing(vk);
       times.sType                = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PRESENT_TIMES_INFO_GOOGLE;
       times.pNext                = NULL;
       times.swapchainCount       = 1;
